# Test: two interfaces trade addresses in one commit, and each address leaves
#       the interface that held it before it arrives on the one that takes it.
#
# zdual0 holds 10.90.0.1/24 and zdual1 holds 10.91.0.1/24. The reloaded config
# gives each address to the other interface. The iface decomposer emits a create
# and a destroy for each one, and the surviving constraint rule orders every
# address removal ahead of every address addition. The bgp decomposer emits a
# stop and a start for peer1, which binds 10.90.0.1, because that address is
# disturbed. So the commit applies in the order the owner asked for: stop the
# binder, remove the addresses, add them back, start the binder
# (docs/architecture/config/apply-ordering.md, "The five phases").
#
# VALIDATES: phases 2 to 5. Each address is removed before the same address is
#            added on the other interface, no interface holds both addresses at
#            any observable point, the BGP peer bound to 10.90.0.1 is stopped
#            before that address leaves zdual0 and started after it arrives on
#            zdual1, and the transaction commits.
# PREVENTS: make-before-break coming back. Until 2026-09-11 this test asserted
#           the opposite: that both creates landed before either destroy, so
#           both addresses sat on the host at once. That was a policy inherited
#           from a 2026-05-27 review table and not the owner's requirement, and
#           the machinery that produced it (tryRelaxCycle, markDualPresence,
#           Params.AllowDual and the constraint rule
#           iface-add-address-before-remove-same-interface) is deleted.
#
# The observable points of phases 3 and 4 are the kernel's own netlink
# notifications, so the driver reads them through `ip monitor` and replays them
# over the known initial state (configApplyOrderingSwapDriver,
# internal/test/fixture/register_config_apply_ordering.go). A poll of `ip addr`
# cannot see the order: the two netlink calls are a few hundred microseconds
# apart.
#
# Phases 2 and 5 reach no kernel object, so the check peer observes them
# instead, and it can do that only because it never closes a connection itself
# (option=linger, see the peer block below). The peer writes
# session-returned.txt once the returned session has re-announced its route,
# and the driver waits for that file before it signals the daemon. The daemon
# therefore finishes its reload, which is what the `sighup reload complete`
# expectation below reads.
#
# DISCRIMINATION: one revert for each half of the file, both WALKED in QEMU on
# 2026-09-11 against the guest's own kernel (Ze runtime kernel 7.2, uid 0). The
# two reverted daemons, the unreverted one and the harness came out of one tree
# window, and the two builds of the unreverted daemon are byte for byte
# identical, so each red and the green are a pair (green md5
# 25dbe432f5e33edea41bd49d912be590,
# tmp/session/2026-09-08-cbc36cee-41ac-4afd-8b71-1bae841964d9/scratch/sw/pairbuild.sh).
#
#   PHASES 2 AND 5. Restore the pre-284620ac2 early return in
#   decomposeBGPOperations (internal/component/bgp/plugin/operation.go), so a
#   root with no diff of its own emits no peer operation. The bgp section here
#   is byte-identical across the reload, so the reverted daemon never stops
#   peer1. RED: the peer held its first connection open for the whole reload,
#   the daemon never closed it, no second connection came, and the driver
#   failed with "the check peer never wrote session-returned.txt: the session
#   bound to 10.90.0.1/24 was not stopped and started around the move, so
#   phases 2 and 5 did not happen". The peer's own log carries the mechanism:
#   "holding this connection open until the remote closes it", and the close
#   that follows it is the test teardown 20 seconds later, not a phase 2.
#
#   PHASES 3 AND 4. Unregister the constraint rule
#   iface-remove-address-before-add-address
#   (internal/component/iface/operation.go), the one rule that orders every
#   address removal ahead of every address addition. The planner then emits its
#   additions first and the swap lands make-before-break. RED: "interface
#   zdual0 held both addresses after add address 10.91.0.1/24 dev zdual0: the
#   move was made before it was broken".
#
# GREEN under the unreverted daemon: 11 of 11 steps pass in 3.2s, the peer
# exchange over both connections among them. Log
# tmp/session/2026-09-08-cbc36cee-41ac-4afd-8b71-1bae841964d9/scratch/sw/walk-2.log,
# and walk-1.log beside it for the same walk one fix earlier, whose peer output
# shows the whole sequence in order: hold,
# "the remote closed the held session", "waiting for next connection (1/2)",
# the new connection from 10.90.0.1, its UPDATE and End-of-RIB, and the rewrite
# that marks phase 5.
#
# The rejected reading is "the swap landed, so the order was right": that is an
# inference from the final state, which carries no order at all, and is why the
# driver asserts from the event stream. The rejected reading for the peer half
# is "two connections happened, so the session restarted": ze dials again on
# its retry timer whoever closed, so a count fences nothing unless the peer is
# incapable of closing first. That is what option=linger buys here.

# needs-linux: dummy interfaces, address assignment and `ip monitor` are kernel
# surfaces, and creating a device plus moving an address needs CAP_NET_ADMIN.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin

# ze-peer holds its session open across the whole reload (option=linger), and
# that is what makes the second connection evidence. The peer never closes, so
# the only thing that can end connection A is the daemon: phase 2 stops the
# session bound to 10.90.0.1, because that address changes interface in this
# commit and the binder holding it is disturbed, and phase 5 starts it again
# once the address has arrived on zdual1
# (docs/architecture/config/apply-ordering.md).
#
# A connection COUNT on its own asserts nothing here, and until 2026-09-11 this
# block was exactly that. ze-peer closed each connection as soon as that
# connection's expectations were met, so the first session was gone before the
# reload began: phase 2 had nothing to stop, and a daemon whose decomposer
# emitted no peer operation at all still reached two connections on ze's own
# retry timer. Measured under that revert, in
# tmp/session/2026-09-08-cbc36cee-41ac-4afd-8b71-1bae841964d9/scratch/dw/walk-4.log.
# Held open, connection B exists only because the session DROPPED and RETURNED.
#
# Before that the block said one connection, which asserted that the session
# never restarted. That was the inherited make-before-break policy: both
# addresses stayed on the host across the swap and the session was left running
# over a binding that had moved underneath it.
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=linger:value=true

# Connection A: the session that held 10.90.0.1 on zdual0, up to the commit.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040A0000014005040000006418C0A801
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000

# Connection B: the same session started again, from the same address on zdual1,
# announcing its route again. The rewrite is how the peer tells the driver that
# phase 5 happened: the driver reads the kernel, and a BGP session is not a
# kernel object, so it waits for session-returned.txt before it signals the
# daemon (awaitSessionReturned, internal/test/fixture/register_config_apply_ordering.go).
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040A0000014005040000006418C0A801
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
action=rewrite:conn=2:seq=1:source=session-returned.src:dest=session-returned.txt
EOF_PEER

# The marker's body, copied to session-returned.txt by the action above. The
# file is the signal; its text is for whoever reads the artifacts after a run.
tmpfs=session-returned.src:terminator=EOF_RETURNED
the session bound to 10.90.0.1 dropped in phase 2 and returned in phase 5
EOF_RETURNED

# Initial config: each interface holds the address the other one takes.
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 10.90.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 10.0.0.1
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 192.168.1.0/24
			}
		}
	}
}

interface {
	dummy zdual0 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.90.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
	dummy zdual1 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.91.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Reloaded config: the two addresses change places. The bgp section is
# byte-identical, so the bgp root does not diff. The transaction carries the
# four interface operations AND the stop and start of peer1, which the bgp
# decomposer emits from the disturbed address set rather than from a diff of
# its own.
tmpfs=config2.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF2
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 10.90.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 10.0.0.1
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 192.168.1.0/24
			}
		}
	}
}

interface {
	dummy zdual0 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.91.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
	dummy zdual1 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.90.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF2

option=env:var=ze.log.interface:value=info

# The driver runs in the FOREGROUND so the runner waits for it, which is what
# keeps the readback and the event stream out of the teardown's way. The same
# reasoning is written out in tx-iface-address-swap.ci, where a background
# driver raced its own teardown.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze-test fixture reload/config-apply-ordering-address-swap-driver:timeout=60s

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=sighup reload complete
expect=stderr:contains=OK: break-before-make observed
expect=file:path=session-returned.txt:exists=true
expect=file:path=swap-final.txt:contains=zdual0=10.91.0.1/24
expect=file:path=swap-final.txt:contains=zdual1=10.90.0.1/24
expect=file:path=swap-final.txt:not-contains=zdual0=10.90.0.1/24
expect=file:path=swap-final.txt:not-contains=zdual1=10.91.0.1/24
expect=file:path=swap-events.txt:exists=true
reject=stderr:contains=operation dependency cycle
reject=stderr:contains=config apply partial failure
reject=stderr:contains=config verify failed
